// W11 gate (the int<->float convert intrinsic) — float_of_int / float_to_int are
// REAL machine conversions (the gradient's coercion arm), NOT identity stubs that
// reinterpret bits. Lower recognizes them by name and emits LConvert(IntToFloat /
// FloatToInt); emit projects the one signed WASM op:
//   - float_of_int(n) -> f64.convert_i32_s  (i32 widened to its f64 value)
//   - float_to_int(f) -> i32.trunc_f64_s    (f64 truncated toward zero to i32)
// The boxed-float / bit-reinterpreting identity stubs (and the entire float_add/
// sub/mul/div/neg/lt/ge wrapper family) are DISSOLVED — the operators ARE the
// float ops (the repr gradient emits f64.* from TFloat operands).
//
// A round-trip proves both directions compose to identity for an exact value:
// float_of_int(7) = 7.0 (a genuine f64, not the bits of 7), and float_to_int of
// that = 7. A non-trivial path: float_of_int(3) * 2.5 = 7.5 (f64.mul on the
// converted value), float_to_int(7.5) = 7 (truncation toward zero).
//
// THE SEED LAGS THIS FORM (the hand-WAT lower has no LConvert recognition and the
// deleted float_of_int/float_to_int defs surface as E_MissingVariable in the
// seed's view — the same census-shadow lag mn-float-arith documents). So the
// VALUE gate runs through MENTL2 (the wheel compiled by the seed, carrying the
// wheel's LConvert emit), NOT the seed directly. Named peer: Hβ.seed.float-convert
// — the seed mirrors the convert intrinsic + the operator-as-float-op gradient
// when next required, the .mn leading until then (PLAN §5).
//
// Expected exit 14: float_to_int(float_of_int(7))=7 + float_to_int(float_of_int(3) * 2.5)=7 = 14.

fn main(q) = {
  let a = float_to_int(float_of_int(7))            // 7  (round-trip identity)
  let b = float_to_int(float_of_int(3) * 2.5)      // 7  (3.0*2.5=7.5 truncated)
  a + b                                             // 14
}
